# labqoat-python

Typed SDK and CLI for the Labqoat starter API. Distribution
`labqoat`, import `labqoat`, commands `lab` and `labqoat`.

## Quick start

```python
from labqoat.client import Client

with Client("http://127.0.0.1:4000", "local", "your-password") as client:
    note = client.create_note("First", body="Hello")
    page = client.list_notes(limit=20)
    for item in client.iter_notes():
        print(item.id, item.title)
```

The CLI defaults to **production** at `https://app.labqoat.com`, which uses a
publicly trusted certificate. Profiles saved with the old tunnel URL
(`https://127.0.0.1:4043`) should be updated, and `SSL_CERT_FILE` no longer needs
to point at the private installation CA. Log in:

```sh
lab auth login --username labqoat
lab status
lab run list
```

Work is grouped into projects: a named container that owns runs, datasets,
dashboards and one research memory. Start in a working directory with:

```sh
lab project init my-benchmark
lab project current
```

`init` creates the project and pins it as `[tool.labqoat] project = "my-benchmark"`
in `./pyproject.toml`, so every command run from there acts on it. `--project NAME`
and `LABQOAT_PROJECT` override the pin; without any of them the project named
`default` is used. `run list`, `dataset list` and `dashboard list` show the selected
project and take `--all-projects`; `lab project move --to NAME --run ID` (also
`--app`, `--dashboard`, `--dataset`) reorganizes existing work. The SDK stays
explicit: `Client(..., project="my-benchmark")` selects the project, and
`labqoat.projects.project_selection.ProjectSelection.resolve().name` applies the
CLI's lookup order in a script.

Use an explicit override for a local instance:

```sh
lab --base-url http://127.0.0.1:4000 status
```

Saved profiles retain their configured endpoints; `--base-url` overrides the
selected profile. Credentials remain scoped to that endpoint. The SDK takes an
explicit URL, as in the local example above.

Credentials use HTTP Basic on every request. Plain HTTP is accepted only
for explicit loopback destinations.

Every `submit` command (`run`, `train`, `eval`, and `import`)
accepts `--watch` to print the submission response and then stream durable
run events, just like the corresponding `watch` command. Evaluations watch
their primary run. Without the flag, submission returns immediately.

```sh
lab run submit my_package:RUN --watch
lab train submit my_training_job --epochs 10 --watch
lab eval submit my_evaluation --dataset team/data@v1 --model team/model@v1 --watch
```

Production GPU workloads can select hardware and a minimum per-device memory:

```python
from labqoat.authoring.resources import Resources

resources = Resources(
    cpu_millicores=4000,
    memory_mb=16384,
    gpu_count=1,
    gpu_model="l40s",
    gpu_memory_mb=44 * 1024,
)
```

Use this resource declaration in the workload's runtime configuration. The
legacy `GpuResources(cpu=4, memory_gib=16, gpu_count=1, gpu_model="l40s",
minimum_gpu_memory_gib=44)` form expresses the same requirements. Omit the model
or use `"any"` to choose any GPU meeting the memory floor. Production enforces
both constraints for workers and model serving, including scale-from-zero.
Memory means **MiB per GPU**, not combined VRAM or fractional GPU allocation.
EC2 reports 22,888 MiB for L4/A10G, 45,776 MiB for L40S, and 81,920 MiB for
H100; marketed 24/48 GB sizes should not be used as 24/48 GiB floors.
The default production pool offers T4, A10G, L4, L40S and H100; configured pool
limits still apply. Unsupported hardware combinations fail capacity admission.

Training submits from source. `lab train submit` packages the exact working
tree, uploads it once, and accepts a run without importing, installing, or
executing the workload on the submitting machine; only the CLI and the source
files are required. The platform then prepares the run — building the image,
compiling the workload contract inside it, resolving dependencies, and
freezing the execution manifest — before scheduling training. Preparation
stages are visible on the run as `preparing` status detail, and `--watch`
follows preparation through training. Heavy third-party imports in workload
modules are supported because inspection runs inside the built image.

Every `logs` command accepts `--follow` to keep printing new log entries.
Run, training, evaluation, and import logs use the existing run
log stream and stop when the run finishes. Inference, replica, worker, node,
and pool logs use one server-sent event (SSE) connection until the server
closes it or you press Ctrl-C. Existing time filters, sort order, limit, and
starting cursor are sent with the initial streaming request. Following does
not poll snapshots. Each `log.entry` event prints its unchanged JSON data as
one line, flushed immediately for pipes; heartbeats are ignored and
`log.error` events report a CLI error. Ctrl-C closes the connection. Without
`--follow`, logs return the same single JSON page as before.

```sh
lab run logs RUN_ID --follow
lab train logs RUN_ID --follow
lab eval logs EVALUATION_ID --follow
lab inference logs DEPLOYMENT_ID --follow
lab inference replica logs REPLICA_ID --follow
lab worker logs WORKER_ID --follow
```

`lab node logs NODE_ID` reads `/api/v1/nodes/item/logs?ref=NODE_ID` once.
The server returns logs from active and retained workloads on that node,
including completed workers and serving replicas. Entries include node and
source metadata and pass through the usual SDK response validation.

With `--follow`, the CLI reads `/api/v1/nodes/item/logs/stream?ref=NODE_ID`
over one SSE connection and prints raw entry JSONL. The server discovers
new workloads and supplies their log entries.

```sh
lab node list
lab node logs NODE_ID
lab node logs NODE_ID --follow
```

`lab pool logs POOL_REF` reads `/api/v1/pools/item/logs?ref=POOL_REF` once,
with the same semantics as node logs across active and retained workloads
in the pool. Entries preserve pool, node, and source attribution through SDK
response validation. With `--follow`, the CLI uses one
`/api/v1/pools/item/logs/stream?ref=POOL_REF` SSE connection and prints raw
entry JSONL as the server discovers workloads and supplies their logs.
Node and pool logs accept only their required reference and `--follow`;
they do not accept cursor, limit, sort, or timestamp filters.

```sh
lab pool list
lab pool logs POOL_REF
lab pool logs POOL_REF --follow
```

## Inference and evaluation

Inference uses ordinary `@app.cls()` declarations bound to immutable Artifacts.
Submit `Predictor(weights=weights).predict` through `client.runs` or
`context.runs`; each Run captures its code, image and constructor inputs.
See [weight-backed inference](../../examples/labqoat-model-inference/README.md).

Evaluation functions submit ordinary inference and scoring Runs, retain typed
prediction datasets, and return `EvaluationReport.complete(...)`. The report
validates exact expected membership, explicit Run attribution, consistent finite
metric names and equal-weight means. It is an ordinary typed result with no
separate publication or completion lifecycle. Rescoring explicitly passes a
previous result and its retained predictions into another function.
See [NAM](../../examples/labqoat-nam-a2/README.md) and
[Jev decision training](../../examples/labqoat-jev/README.md).

## Development

Use `lab --version`, `labqoat --version`, or `bun run cli -- --version` to
identify the executing installation. Editable installs and direct source
execution report the package version, current Git revision, and absolute
Python package path, for example:

```text
labqoat 0.1.0+local.g0123456789ab (/path/to/labqoat/packages/labqoat-python)
```

Tracked changes inside that package add `.dirty` after the revision. If Git
is unavailable or the source has no commit, the revision is `unknown` and the
source path is still shown. Standard wheel installs report only their package
version, such as `labqoat 0.1.0`. Version output works offline without loading
profiles or credentials and does not depend on the current directory.

- `uv sync --locked` — reproducible environment (Python 3.13 development;
  3.11 minimum supported).
- `uv run --locked pytest tests -q --testmon` — affected class-based unit tests.
- `ruff format` / `ruff check` / strict `mypy` — same gates for source,
  tests, support, and generated code.
- `src/labqoat/_generated/` is owned by the schema emitter; never hand-edit.
